Where the robust formula comes from
Start from \;\hat\beta_1 = \beta_1 + \dfrac{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)u_i}{SST_x}\; and take the variance:

\text{Var}(\hat\beta_1) = \frac{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^2\,\sigma_i^2}{SST_x^2}
\qquad\xrightarrow{\ \text{if } \sigma_i^2=\sigma^2\ }\qquad
\frac{\sigma^2}{SST_x}

The usual standard error estimates the right-hand expression, which is why it is wrong when the \sigma_i^2 differ.

We cannot estimate n separate variances \sigma_i^2. White’s (1980) insight: we do not have to, because only the sum matters. Replacing each \sigma_i^2 by the squared residual \hat u_i^2 gives a consistent estimator of that sum:

\widehat{\text{Var}}(\hat\beta_1) = \frac{\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^2\,\hat u_i^2}{SST_x^2}

Each \hat u_i^2 is a hopeless estimate of its own \sigma_i^2, but the average behaves well as n grows.

Cigarette demand: OLS vs. FGLS
OLS

\begin{aligned} \widehat{cigs} &= - \underset{\small (24.08)}{3.64} + \underset{\small (0.728)}{0.880}\,\log(income) - \underset{\small (5.77)}{0.751}\,\log(cigpric) - \underset{\small (0.167)}{0.501}\,educ \\ & + \underset{\small (0.160)}{0.771}\,age - \underset{\small (0.00174)}{0.00902}\,age^2 - \underset{\small (1.11)}{2.83}\,restaurn \end{aligned}

FGLS (weighted least squares with weights 1/\hat h_i)

\begin{aligned} \widehat{cigs} &=  \underset{\small (17.80)}{5.64} + \underset{\small (0.437)}{1.30}\,\log(income) - \underset{\small (4.46)}{2.94}\,\log(cigpric) - \underset{\small (0.120)}{0.463}\,educ \\ & + \underset{\small (0.0968)}{0.482}\,age - \underset{\small (0.000939)}{0.00563}\,age^2 - \underset{\small (0.796)}{3.46}\,restaurn \end{aligned}

A single dummy variable

wage = \beta_0 + \delta_0\, female + \beta_1\, educ + u

Take conditional expectations for each group (with E(u\mid \cdot) = 0):

\begin{aligned}
E(wage \mid female = 1, educ) &= (\beta_0 + \delta_0) + \beta_1 educ\\
E(wage \mid female = 0, educ) &= \beta_0 + \beta_1 educ
\end{aligned}



\boxed{\;\delta_0 = E(wage\mid female=1, educ) - E(wage\mid female=0, educ)\;}

\delta_0 is the difference in intercepts: the wage gap between women and men at the same level of education. The slope \beta_1 is the same for both groups, so the two lines are parallel.

Why OLS is consistent, and when it is not
Simple regression: substitute y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + u_i and divide numerator and denominator by n:

\hat\beta_1 = \beta_1 + \frac{n^{-1}\sum_i (x_i - \bar x)\,u_i}{n^{-1}\sum_i (x_i - \bar x)^2}
\;\xrightarrow{\;p\;}\; \beta_1 + \frac{\text{Cov}(x, u)}{\text{Var}(x)}



Under MLR.4′, \text{Cov}(x, u) = 0, so \text{plim}\,\hat\beta_1 = \beta_1.
Otherwise the asymptotic bias (inconsistency) is \;\text{Cov}(x_1, u)/\text{Var}(x_1).



Omitted variable: if y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \beta_2 x_2 + v and we omit x_2:

\text{plim}\,\tilde\beta_1 = \beta_1 + \beta_2\,\delta_1, \qquad \delta_1 = \frac{\text{Cov}(x_1, x_2)}{\text{Var}(x_1)}

Increasing the sample size does not cure inconsistency. \tilde\beta_1 just gets closer and closer to the wrong value.

Sums of squares
Every observation splits into a fitted part and a residual part: \;y_i = \hat y_i + \hat u_i



\begin{aligned}
SST &= \textstyle\sum_{i}(y_i - \bar y)^2 && \text{total}\\
SSE &= \textstyle\sum_{i}(\hat y_i - \bar y)^2 && \text{explained}\\
SSR &= \textstyle\sum_{i}\hat u_i^2 && \text{residual}
\end{aligned}



\boxed{\,SST = SSE + SSR\,}

Note that \widehat{\text{Var}}(y) = SST/(n-1).


Goodness of fit: the coefficient of determination

R^2 = \frac{SSE}{SST} = 1 - \frac{SSR}{SST} = \big[\text{Corr}(y,\hat y)\big]^2, \qquad 0 \le R^2 \le 1

R^2 is the fraction of the sample variation in y that is explained by x. Multiplied by 100, it is a percentage.

Where that identity comes from
The simple regression slope is \;\tilde\beta_1 = \dfrac{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)\,y_i}{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)^2}. Write y_i with the multiple regression fit, y_i = \hat\beta_0 + \hat\beta_1x_{i1} + \hat\beta_2x_{i2} + \hat u_i:

\tilde\beta_1 = \frac{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)\big(\hat\beta_0 + \hat\beta_1 x_{i1} + \hat\beta_2 x_{i2} + \hat u_i\big)}{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)^2}


Three OLS algebra facts do the work: \sum_i(x_{i1}-\bar x_1) = 0 kills the intercept, \sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)\hat u_i = 0 kills the residual term, and the \hat\beta_1 term leaves exactly \hat\beta_1. What survives is

\tilde\beta_1 = \hat\beta_1 + \hat\beta_2\,\underbrace{\frac{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)x_{i2}}{\sum_i (x_{i1}-\bar x_1)^2}}_{\tilde\delta_1\;=\;\text{slope of } x_2 \text{ on } x_1}

This is a sample identity — no assumptions yet. Take expectations and the same algebra gives the omitted variable bias formula on the next slides.

The F statistic

F = \frac{(SSR_r - SSR_{ur})/q}{SSR_{ur}/(n-k-1)} \;\sim\; F_{q,\,n-k-1} \quad\text{under } H_0 \text{ (CLM assumptions)}




q = number of restrictions = numerator df = df_r - df_{ur}
n - k - 1 = denominator df, from the unrestricted model
F \geq 0 always
Reject H_0 if F &gt; c, with c from F_{q,\,n-k-1}



R^2 form
Using SSR = SST(1-R^2):

F = \frac{(R^2_{ur} - R^2_r)/q}{(1 - R^2_{ur})/(n-k-1)}

Valid only when both models have the same dependent variable.










Same sample!


Both models must be estimated on the same observations. If some observations have missing values only for the excluded variables, drop them from the restricted model too.

What maximum likelihood does
OLS minimizes the sum of squared residuals. That makes no sense here: the model does not predict y_i, it assigns a probability to what we observe.

f(y_i \mid \mathbf x_i;\boldsymbol\beta) =
\underbrace{[G(\mathbf x_i\boldsymbol\beta)]^{y_i}}_{\text{used when } y_i = 1}
\cdot
\underbrace{[1 - G(\mathbf x_i\boldsymbol\beta)]^{1-y_i}}_{\text{used when } y_i = 0}


Observations are independent, so the probability of the whole sample is the product of these terms. Taking logs turns it into a sum:

\log L(\boldsymbol\beta) = \sum_{i=1}^n \Big\{ y_i \log G(\mathbf x_i\boldsymbol\beta) + (1-y_i)\log\big[1 - G(\mathbf x_i\boldsymbol\beta)\big] \Big\}

The maximum likelihood estimator \hat{\boldsymbol\beta} is the value that makes the data we actually observed as likely as possible. There is no formula for it: R maximizes \log L numerically. Every term is a log of a probability, so \log L &lt; 0 and “bigger” means “less negative”.

RESET: hypotheses and the test statistic

H_0: \textcolor{#d9622b}{\gamma_1} = \textcolor{#c0392b}{\gamma_2} = 0 \qquad\text{(the functional form is correct)}


H_1: \textcolor{#d9622b}{\gamma_1} \neq 0 \;\text{ or }\; \textcolor{#c0392b}{\gamma_2} \neq 0 \qquad\text{(neglected nonlinearity: the model is misspecified)}


With q = 2 restrictions and k+2 regressors in the unrestricted model:

F = \frac{(\text{SSR}_r - \text{SSR}_{ur})/2}{\text{SSR}_{ur}/(n-k-3)} \;=\; \frac{(R^2_{ur} - R^2_{r})/2}{(1-R^2_{ur})/(n-k-3)} \;\stackrel{H_0}{\sim}\; F_{2,\,n-k-3}

A large F (small p-value) is evidence against the functional form. Note H_1 does not say which form is right.
